This time: monocular VSLAM on the Málaga Stereo and Laser Urban Data Set, a car driven through the streets of Málaga, with high-rate rectified stereo, LiDAR and GPS. A 2014 classic of urban SLAM benchmarking: sunlight, traffic, pedestrians, and long drives with plenty of chances to drift.
🌐 Dataset: https://www.mrpt.org/MalagaUrbanDataset

I ran AllFeature-VSLAM in monocular mode, tightly coupling two feature types from opposite ends of a decade of research:

🌀 A-KAZE: a handcrafted classic, detecting and describing features in nonlinear scale space.
🔗 https://github.com/pablofdezalc/akaze

🧠 ALIKED: a modern learned feature, with deformable convolutions and a lightweight descriptor network.
🔗 https://github.com/Shiaoming/ALIKED

Handcrafted and learned features, matched and optimised together in the same monocular pipeline, that is what the AllFeature coupling is for.

The whole experiment is one command: pixi run demo allfeature-dev malaga 01

The Málaga integration into VSLAM-LAB was, once again, one CLAUDE skill: point it at the dataset page and the download, extraction, rgb, intrinsics and ground truth come out in the VSLAM-LAB format, ready for every system in the benchmark.

Thanks to José-Luis Blanco-Claraco, Francisco-Ángel Moreno-Dueñas and Javier González-Jiménez for building and open-sourcing the Málaga Urban Dataset. 📣 This integration was done by CLAUDE and me, if you spot anything we got wrong, or any way to squeeze more out of your dataset, please reach out or make a pull request.

To Pablo Fernández Alcantarilla and co-authors for A-KAZE, and to Xiaoming Zhao and co-authors for ALIKED.

And to the ORB-SLAM2 authors, AllFeature-VSLAM builds upon their work.

